-- statement:
--   For coordinate metrics $g_1,g_2$ on $\mathbb R^4$, sets $U,V\subseteq\mathbb R^4$, maps $\Phi,\Psi:\mathbb R^4\to\mathbb R^4$ and a matrix-valued function $J$, the proposition $\mathrm{CoordRelation}(g_1,g_2,U,V,\Phi,\Psi,J)$ is the conjunction of: $\Psi(\Phi(x))=x$ for $x\in U$; $\Phi(\Psi(y))=y$ for $y\in V$; $\Phi$ maps $U$ bijectively onto $V$; $\Phi$ is $C^\infty$ on $U$; $\Psi$ is $C^\infty$ on $V$; for all $x\in U$ and indices $k,i$ the slice $u\mapsto\Phi(x[x_i:=u])_k$ has derivative $J(x)_{ki}$ at $u=x_i$; and $$J(x)^{\mathsf T}\,g_2(\Phi(x))\,J(x)=g_1(x)\quad(x\in U).$$ Row $k$ of $J$ is the output component, column $i$ the input coordinate.
--
